Z||000000||paulg||10-08-1999||02:08 PM||||It's down to the wire, I must make a choice. Do I put a 6 cyl. engine in my 86 SE, or just replace the blown 4 cyl. with another? Also, do I spend the extra $400 for the Jasper engine, or do I use the Engine factory recommended by another Fiero Owner?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||10-08-1999||03:21 PM||||Unless you have a v6 parts car or have all the stuff from a v6 donor, you will have a hard time putting a v6 in the place of the 4cyl. A lot of stuff must be changed for this swap. Some items include: fuel pump, ECM and all engine control wiring-the big harness that attaches the ECM to the engine!, throttle cable, tachometer, all engine accessary brackets, the dog-bone mount. . .Is it worth the trouble without a donor car? I have a v6 GT parts car with a low mileage replacement engine and auto trans that I will sell for $500. It is running, just the body and interior are really used up. I have thought about putting the engine in my Duke powered se, but I am going to hold out for an 88 GT or Formula.||||reg||13
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||10-08-1999||04:50 PM||||I once had a perfectly good running 86 SE that had been hit a few times. I decided to search for a cheap 88 Formula, GT or Coupe to build 1 good Fiero from the 2. I found an 88 coupe and put the V6 in it. If I did not have the entire donor car it would have been impossible to do the swap. ||||reg||3
